---
title: Data subject requests
description: Handling access, erasure, and consent under GDPR and LGPD.
section: Compliance
---

Under GDPR and LGPD, a person can ask you what data you hold about them, and can ask you to delete
it. GateKeeper gives you both, on the **Privacy** tab of a user's detail page.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> These tools cover the data *GateKeeper* holds — identity, credentials, sessions, login history,
> consents. They know nothing about the data your own application stores about that user. A complete
> response to a data subject request almost always means combining a GateKeeper export with an
> export from your own systems.

## Export a user's data

**Users → (a user) → Privacy → Export**. Produces a machine-readable dump of everything GateKeeper
holds for that person, which is what you hand over in response to an access request.

## Erase a user

Also on the Privacy tab. This is the right-to-be-forgotten path.

> [!CAUTION]
> Erasure is irreversible, and it is not the same as deactivating an account. There is no undo, and
> no support tooling to bring the user back — GateKeeper genuinely no longer has their data. Export
> first if there is any chance you will need the record.

Erasure does not remove the *fact* that actions occurred from the audit log. That is deliberate and
generally the correct reading of the regulation: a tamper-evident record that something happened is
not the same as continuing to process someone's personal data, and destroying your audit trail on
request would be its own compliance failure. If you have a specific legal opinion that says
otherwise for your jurisdiction, that is a conversation to have with counsel, not a setting.

## Policy acceptances

The Privacy tab also shows which versions of your terms and privacy policy this user accepted, and
when.

This is what turns "they agreed to the terms" into something you can actually evidence. Acceptance
is tracked **per version** — so when you publish new terms, you can tell who has accepted the new
ones and who is still on the old. Versions are gated by the `POLICY_TERMS_VERSION` and
`POLICY_PRIVACY_VERSION` settings on the server. See [Legal pages](/docs/legal-pages).

## Consents

Which applications this user has granted access to, and the ability to revoke one. Revoking a
consent means the next authorization for that client shows the consent screen again.

## Retention purge

**Compliance → Compliance** has a **purge** action, which deletes expired refresh tokens, dead
sessions, and spent authorization codes.

This is data-minimisation housekeeping: material that has already expired and serves no purpose is
not data you should still be holding. Purging is safe — by definition nothing it removes is still
valid — and it is recorded in the audit log as `data.purged`.
